fails: The list goes up, and {Ns} name is not on it.
fails: A twisted knee halfway through ends the trial for {N}.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life events

== your first real job
tier: life event
at: employer
stages: juvenile
age: 14-19
per_year: 0 .15 0 0 0 0
drivers: prosper+.4 ties+.3 health+.2 money+.2 unrest-.3
times: once, usually between fifteen and eighteen: about 15 in 100 teenagers a year start a part-time job (D13, data)
likelier: a growing economy, family or friends who know someone hiring, a town with cafés, shops and farms, good health
rarer: a recession, unrest, poor health, no money for the fares or the clothes the job needs, strict rules on child labour, long school days, a family that forbids it
once: yes
excludes: career
domain: career1
rite: young_adult
tone: mixed
life: work, money
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague, parent, friend
earth: your first real job with a contract and a payslip: a café, a supermarket, a farm in summer, a building site
scene: {N} has a job: a name badge, a rota on the staffroom wall, and a first payslip with their name printed on it.
scene W: {N} signs the contract, reads the staff handbook cover to cover, and turns up fifteen minutes early on the first day.
scene U: Everything is new: the till, the stock, the way {boss} talks to customers. {N} wants to learn all of it.
scene B: {N} earns their own money now, and nobody can tell them how to spend it. That is a kind of power.
scene R: The first shift is noise, rush and aching feet, and {N} walks out at closing time feeling grown up.
scene G: {parent} started work at the same age, in the same kind of place. Some things are handed down like an old coat.
- W | be on time for every shift, and do every task by the book | v: conformity | tags: commit:career | chance: 85
- U | learn how the whole business works, from the stockroom to the accounts | v: self-direction, achievement | mark: learned a skill | tags: commit:career | chance: 68
- B | make yourself indispensable, then ask for more hours and better pay | v: achievement, power | tags: commit:career | chance: 76
- R | throw yourself into it, love the rush, and make friends with the whole crew | v: stimulation | mark: made a friend | tags: commit:career | chance: 88
- G | settle in, keep your head down, and stay, the way your family always has | v: tradition, security | tags: commit:career | chance: 68
- BG | work every hour you can get and save every penny, like a squirrel before winter | habit | v: security, achievement | chance: 78
- BR | quit the moment it gets boring, and blow the pay on a good time | v: hedonism | mark: turned down a chance | chance: 20
- UR | come up with a better way to run your section, and pitch it to the boss | door | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 45
- WG | hand most of your first pay to your family, as is expected | identity | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 94
- WU | learn your rights at work and make sure every payslip is right | v: security, universalism (justice) | chance: 92
works: Within a month {boss} is asking for {N} by name on the busy shifts.
works: {N} opens a bank account and watches it slowly grow.
fails: {N} is let go after a few weeks, with no reason given.
fails: The shifts eat into everything else, and school starts to slip.

== a close friend moves away
tier: life event
stages: juvenile
age: 10-18
per_year: 0 .1 0 0 0 0
drivers: ties+.3 unrest+.3 prosper+.2
gap: 2-4
times: once or twice in the teenage years (estimate: Part D has no entry for a friend's move; leans on D37, about 7 to 10 in 100 families with children moving each year, and a teenager having several close friends)
likelier: a mobile country, rented homes, parents in the forces or on short contracts, a boom elsewhere, unrest
rarer: villages where families stay for generations, owned homes, settled times
tone: trouble
life: friends
horizon: months
roles: friend, parent, the old group
earth: your best friend's family is moving to another city at the end of the month
scene: {friend}'s family is moving to another city at the end of the month. The boxes are already stacked in the hall.
scene W: {N} and {friend} always said they would be friends for ever. Now the promise will have to hold across three hundred miles.
scene U: {N} looks up the distance, the train times and the price of a ticket. There must be a way to make this work.
scene B: With {friend} gone, the group will need someone new at its centre. {N} notices the thought and does not quite push it away.
scene R: It is unfair, it is too soon, and nobody asked {N} what they thought.
scene G: People move like birds and seasons. {N} helps carry the boxes and tries to remember everything.
- W | promise to write every week, and keep that promise | v: benevolence | mark: kept your word | chance: 45
- U | plan the visits: train times, savings, the summer holidays | v: benevolence | chance: 62
- B | help them sell what they cannot take, for a fair cut | v: power (resources), achievement | chance: 75
- R | skip school to spend their whole last day together | v: benevolence, hedonism | closed: approval: truancy; backfire: detention, and a row at home | chance: 85
- G | help them pack, say a proper goodbye, and let them go | v: benevolence | chance: 80
- RG | spend one last wild night camping out together before the move | body: light | v: stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | closed: approval: out all night; backfire: caught, and grounded for the last week | chance: 80
- UB | keep tabs on their new life online, and on whoever replaces you | v: security | act: keep tabs on their friend's new life online, and on whoever replaces them | tech: internet | chance: 42
- UG | bury a time capsule together, to dig up in ten years | binds | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 90
- WB | throw the farewell party yourself, and step into their place in the group | v: power, benevolence | chance: 65
- WR | get the whole group out to wave them off on moving day, with a huge banner | identity | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 85
works: The messages keep coming, and the friendship holds across the miles.
works: Missing {friend} slowly makes room for new people in {Ns} life.
fails: The messages thin out, and by spring they have stopped.
fails: {N} spends a long, lonely term eating lunch alone.

== money runs out at home
tier: life event
stages: juvenile
age: 10-18
per_year: 0 .04 0 0 0 0
drivers: money-.6 prosper-.5 unrest+.4 health-.2
gap: 3-6
times: once or twice in a childhood for many families: serious debt crises reach about 3 to 5 in 100 households a year in the parents' working years (D59, estimate; D59's own juvenile rate is zero because it counts a person's own bankruptcy), and a parent's job loss (D57) is the usual start
likelier: one income, insecure work, a parent's job loss, a recession, illness and medical bills, a divorce, gambling or drinking at home, a big family, debt
rarer: savings, two steady incomes, health insurance, a welfare state, relatives who can help, a growing economy
rite: young_adult
tone: trouble
life: family, money
horizon: months
roles: parent, sibling, grandparent, friend, neighbours
earth: a parent loses their job, the bills pile up, and the fridge gets emptier every week
scene: {parent} has lost their job. The heating stays off until evening, the shopping is all the cheapest brands, and letters pile up unopened on the mat.
scene W: At night {N} hears {parent} on the phone, apologising to someone about a bill. Everyone in this house will have to do their part.
scene U: {N} finds the bank letters in a drawer and reads them twice. The numbers are bad, but numbers can be worked with.
scene B: {friend} has new trainers again. {N} decides they are done waiting for money to come to them.
scene R: {N} is furious, at the company, at the bank, at the whole unfair world, and nobody at home will say it out loud.
scene G: {grandparent} talks about the hard winters of their own childhood, when everyone went without and got through together.
- W | cut back on everything without complaint, and help keep the house running | v: conformity, benevolence | chance: 70
- U | compare the prices of everything the family buys, and find where to save | v: security | mark: learned a skill | chance: 82
- B | do cash jobs for the neighbours, and keep a cut for yourself | v: achievement, power | chance: 80
- R | keep the house laughing: games, music and silly nights in for everyone | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 65
- G | help grow vegetables and cook cheap meals from whatever is in the cupboard | habit | v: security, benevolence | mark: learned a skill | chance: 70
- WUR | start a Saturday car wash with friends, with a rota, prices and a sign | v: achievement, benevolence | chance: 72
- WUB | lend your parent your savings, and write down exactly what they owe you | binds | v: security, power | act: lend their parent their savings, and write down exactly what the parent owes them | chance: 72
- WRG | tell your friends straight out how things are, and share what you all have | identity | v: benevolence | mark: made a friend | chance: 72
- UBG | find things in the house worth selling, and haggle hard for the best price | v: security, achievement | closed: approval: selling family things without asking; backfire: a row when a parent misses something | chance: 70
- BRG | shoplift food when the cupboards are bare | v: security, benevolence | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: theft; backfire: caught, and a police caution | chance: 70
works: The family gets through the worst of it, and {N} learns what they are made of.
works: {parent} finds new work, and the heating comes back on.
fails: Things get worse before they get better, and there is talk of losing the house.
fails: The strain shows in every row at the dinner table.

== you win a scholarship or a place
tier: life event
at: school
stages: juvenile
age: 11-18
per_year: 0 .05 0 0 0 0
drivers: community+.3 ties+.3 money+.2 prosper+.2 unrest-.3 fortune+.1
times: about 5 in 100 teenagers a year win a scholarship or a selective place (estimate scaled down from D22, which counts any award or prize at about 10 in 100; contests alone, D23, reach 8 in 100)
likelier: good schools and clubs within reach, friends and a community that notice talent, a family that pushes, money for fees and travel, means-tested scholarships, a talent spotted early
rarer: no selective schools or clubs nearby, a family that cannot pay the fees or the travel, a teenager who keeps to themselves, war or displacement
rite: young_adult
tone: joy
life: school
horizon: years
roles: parent, mentor, friend, rival
earth: a letter says you have won a place: a scholarship to a better school, a youth academy, a national youth orchestra
scene: The letter comes on a Tuesday: {N} has won a scholarship to a school two towns away. New uniform, early buses, and everyone left behind.
scene W: {parent} reads the letter three times and then puts it in a frame. {N} feels the weight of everyone's pride.
scene U: The new school has laboratories, a real library, and teachers who have written books. {N} reads the brochure until it falls apart.
scene B: A place like that opens doors that stay shut for everyone else. {N} intends to walk through every one of them.
scene R: {N} screams, jumps on the bed, and phones everyone they have ever met.
scene G: {N} thinks of the friends from the street, the walk to the old school, the corner shop. Not all of it will come along.
- W | accept, and write proper thank-you letters to everyone who helped | v: conformity, benevolence | chance: 72
- U | read everything about the new school and arrive already ahead | v: achievement | chance: 72
- B | take it, and drop the old friends who would hold you back | identity | v: achievement, power | act: take it, and drop the old friends who would hold them back | chance: 68
- R | ring everyone you know, and celebrate as loudly as you can | v: hedonism | chance: 78
- G | turn it down, and stay with your friends and family where you belong | identity | v: tradition, security | mark: turned down a chance | closed: approval: turning down the family's hopes; backfire: a long row at home | chance: 60
- WUG | accept, and keep helping your old friends with their homework after school | v: benevolence | chance: 55
- WBR | take the place, and race to the top of the new class in the first term | v: achievement | chance: 30
- WBG | accept for the family's sake, and carry their hopes like a debt to repay | binds | v: tradition, achievement | chance: 65
- URG | take it, and learn to fend for yourself on the long journey each day | door | v: self-direction | chance: 82
- UBR | bluff that you have another offer, and hold out for a better one | v: power, achievement | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 25
works: The first term goes better than {N} dared to hope.
works: {N} finds people at the new place who think the way they do.
fails: {N} feels out of place among richer, sharper kids, and dreads every morning bus.
fails: The old friends drift away, and the new ones have not arrived yet.

== a trip far from home
tier: life event
at: school
stages: child juvenile young_adult
age: 12-19
per_year: .02 .03 .05 0 0 0
drivers: money+.5 prosper+.3 health+.2 era+.2 unrest-.4
gap: 2-4
times: once or twice in the school years for those who go: about 2 in 100 children, 3 in 100 teenagers and 5 in 100 young adults a year make the journey of a lifetime (D29, estimate)
likelier: money, good health, a school with exchanges, relatives abroad, cheap travel, few duties at home, peace and open borders
rarer: poverty, poor health, caring duties at home, a family that never travels, closed borders, war
rite: young_adult
tone: joy
life: travel, door
horizon: week
roles: friend, a teacher, a host family
earth: a school exchange or a trip abroad: a new country, a new language, two weeks far from home
scene: Two weeks on an exchange in another country: a host family, a language {N} barely speaks, and food that smells like nothing at home.
scene W: The school sent a list of rules and the host family's address. {N} wants to be a good guest and a credit to the school.
scene U: Street signs, menus, the way people greet each other: everything is a puzzle, and {N} wants to solve all of it.
scene B: Nobody here knows who {N} is back home. For two weeks {N} can be anyone they like.
scene R: The plane drops through the clouds onto a city {N} has only seen in pictures, and {Ns} face is pressed to the window.
scene G: The air smells different, the trees are different, the light is different. {N} misses home and loves it here, both at once.
- W | follow the group's rules and be a model guest in your host family's home | door | v: conformity, benevolence | chance: 88
- U | speak only their language all fortnight, however badly | door | v: self-direction, achievement | mark: learned a skill | chance: 62
- B | bring sweets from home and sell them at three times the price | v: achievement, power | chance: 58
- R | slip away from the group to explore the city on your own | door | v: stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | closed: approval: leaving the group; backfire: lost, and sent home early | chance: 80
- G | spend your days with the host family's grandparents, learning how they live | door | v: tradition, universalism (tolerance) | mark: made a friend | chance: 72
- W>B | report the friends who broke curfew, so the teachers pick you to lead next time | v: achievement, power | closed: approval: telling on friends; backfire: shunned for the rest of the trip | act: report the friends who broke curfew, so the teachers pick them to lead next time | chance: 30
- U>R | research the city's hidden corners and lead your friends there after curfew | v: stimulation | closed: approval: out after curfew; backfire: the trip cut short | chance: 65
- B>G | smuggle home a local food that customs forbid, as a present for your family | v: benevolence | closed: law: customs rules; backfire: confiscated at the airport, and a fine | chance: 75
- R>W | throw yourself into the host family's festival as if you were one of them | v: universalism (tolerance) | chance: 92
- G>U | walk the countryside with your host and learn the name of everything in their language | door | v: self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 75
works: {N} comes home with a new language half in their head and a friend on the other side of the world.
works: The trip changes how {N} sees home, in ways that take years to notice.
fails: {N} is homesick and sick from the food, and counts the days.
fails: A row with the host family sours the whole fortnight.

